00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:41.230
So our applications,
our infrastructure,

00:06:41.230 --> 00:06:44.870
is becoming more and
more event-driven.

00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:47.740
So we're interested
in events that

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:51.670
are being emitted by some kind
of service, application, API,

00:06:51.670 --> 00:06:55.360
et cetera, that are
intercepted, here, in our case,

00:06:55.360 --> 00:06:59.620
by Cloud Functions, which is
going to respond to events.

00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:03.460
Inside our function, we
can also trigger new events

00:07:03.460 --> 00:07:08.920
or invoke other services,
APIs, store some data

00:07:08.920 --> 00:07:16.590
in Cloud Datastore, a file
in Cloud Storage, et cetera.

00:07:16.590 --> 00:07:19.110
The unit of work
for Cloud Functions

00:07:19.110 --> 00:07:21.500
is, as the name
implies, a function.

00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:26.370
So a function, it's a small
unit of work with a fine grain

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:28.470
small focus.

00:07:28.470 --> 00:07:32.490
There are two ways to
trigger your function, so

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:37.670
either synchronous calls with
HTTP. or just a mere HTTP.

00:07:37.670 --> 00:07:43.000
or get, or post, or et cetera,
or a more asynchronous events.

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:46.410
So, for example,
new files stored

00:07:46.410 --> 00:07:51.180
in Cloud Storage
or a new message

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:56.250
on a Pub/Sub topic which
can trigger your function.

00:07:56.250 --> 00:08:01.380
Some examples of where you can
use functions-- so typically,

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:04.850
one example I'm going to take
with the HTTP call, that's

00:08:04.850 --> 00:08:07.680
if you're building or
chat bot, for example,

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:11.770
with Dialogflow for
the Google Assistant.

00:08:11.770 --> 00:08:17.910
You can define a WebHook
within the Dialogflow UI,

00:08:17.910 --> 00:08:21.780
to say, OK, when there's
some need for business logic,

00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:26.280
for fetching some data or
something from the chat bot,

00:08:26.280 --> 00:08:29.200
then call this function.

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:34.049
Similarly, if you're using
GitHub for your source code,

00:08:34.049 --> 00:08:36.210
there are commit
hooks which allow

00:08:36.210 --> 00:08:40.350
you to be notified when
there's a new pull requests,

00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:44.610
when there's a new commit
coming up in your source tree.

00:08:44.610 --> 00:08:48.442
So that's for the
synchronous calls with HTTP.

00:08:48.442 --> 00:08:50.400
But another example, and
that's what we're also

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:54.240
going to use in the demo later
on, for example, with pictures

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:56.550
being uploaded to Cloud Storage.

00:08:56.550 --> 00:09:01.140
So you want to create thumbnails
you want to crop the picture

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:04.170
or do something
with that picture

00:09:04.170 --> 00:09:06.920
once it's stored in
Storage, your function

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:09.720
is notified-- it is triggered.

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:12.480
In the demo, as you
shall see, we're

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:16.920
going to use the newly
announced, Cloud Scheduler.

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:21.860
So whenever, at regular
intervals of time--

00:09:21.860 --> 00:09:24.900
let's say, in my case,
I'm using Cloud Scheduler

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:28.800
for triggering an event every
one minute-- every minute.

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:32.014
So I'm going to get a little
message about different ways

00:09:32.014 --> 00:09:33.180
of using the Cloud Schedule.

00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:40.020
But in my case, I'm going to use
the Pub/Sub mechanism or a pipe

00:09:40.020 --> 00:09:45.210
to get the message to
trigger one of my functions.

00:09:45.210 --> 00:09:47.850
And all that, obviously-- oh,
yeah, another example that it

00:09:47.850 --> 00:09:49.620
was interesting that
was given yesterday,

00:09:49.620 --> 00:09:52.800
I think, by Oren Teich,
that was, for example,

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:56.890
with Stackdriver you can create
alerts when something happens.

00:09:56.890 --> 00:10:00.420
For example, you can see
by looking through the logs

00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:03.600
that something happens
that is unusual,

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:08.115
you could also specify that
the alert pings your function.

00:10:08.115 --> 00:10:09.840
So that's an
interesting use case

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:13.000
for further automating
your infrastructure,

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:15.850
your whole project.

00:10:15.850 --> 00:10:22.920
So from a developer
perspective, what

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:26.140
do you deploy is really
just code basically.

00:10:26.140 --> 00:10:27.690
So as a developer,
you just focus--

00:10:27.690 --> 00:10:29.890
and who's a developer
in the audience?

00:10:29.890 --> 00:10:30.960
Yeah, quite a few hands.

00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:32.950
Nice.

00:10:32.950 --> 00:10:35.520
So if you were a developer,
you focused on your code

00:10:35.520 --> 00:10:37.860
and you don't necessarily
want, upfront,

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:40.530
in particular, to have
to think about servers,

00:10:40.530 --> 00:10:42.750
about creating
clusters, about managing

00:10:42.750 --> 00:10:45.300
those servers, or the
provisioning, or all

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:46.430
that stuff.

00:10:46.430 --> 00:10:51.090
Also, at some point, if your
application is very successful,

00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:54.330
you're going to need
to be able to scale.

00:10:54.330 --> 00:10:59.220
And, well, it's not always
easy to scale your architecture

00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:04.150
to cope with the incoming
traffic and traffic spikes.

00:11:04.150 --> 00:11:08.910
But also, a nice aspect
of serverless, it's auto

00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:10.870
scaling down.

00:11:10.870 --> 00:11:13.050
So from zero to n, that's nice.

00:11:13.050 --> 00:11:16.200
But also, from n back
to 1 or 0, that's

00:11:16.200 --> 00:11:18.990
also something that's
important to do.

00:11:18.990 --> 00:11:25.590
And, of course, you don't want
to pay for nothing, basically.

00:11:25.590 --> 00:11:29.290
So if your code is not running,
you're not going to be billed.

00:11:29.290 --> 00:11:31.800
So, yeah.

00:11:31.800 --> 00:11:36.170
Pay only for what
you use, right?

00:11:36.170 --> 00:11:41.190
From your functions-- so I
mentioned a few triggers,

00:11:41.190 --> 00:11:43.160
Cloud Storage,
Pub/Sub, et cetera.

00:11:43.160 --> 00:11:46.410
But from your functions you can
call all the services you want.

00:11:46.410 --> 00:11:48.350
So you have, at your
disposal, things

00:11:48.350 --> 00:11:53.940
like virus kind of databases,
data store, spanner, BigTable,

00:11:53.940 --> 00:11:55.100
et cetera.

00:11:55.100 --> 00:11:57.380
You have access to all the
machine learning APIs--

00:11:57.380 --> 00:11:59.838
that's what we're going to do
with the demo with the Vision

00:11:59.838 --> 00:12:00.980
API.

00:12:00.980 --> 00:12:04.940
You take advantage of all
the logging capabilities,

00:12:04.940 --> 00:12:07.640
error reporting, of Stackdriver.

00:12:07.640 --> 00:12:13.610
So it's one big bag
of things that you

00:12:13.610 --> 00:12:18.040
can take advantage of within
the code of your functions.

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:27.380
So as you learned
about yesterday,

00:12:27.380 --> 00:12:32.840
Cloud Functions is generally
available, with a 99.5% service

00:12:32.840 --> 00:12:33.690
level agreement.

00:12:33.690 --> 00:12:36.320
It's also available
across different regions.

00:12:36.320 --> 00:12:40.760
Four regions-- two in the US,
one in Europe, one in Asia.

00:12:40.760 --> 00:12:44.330
What's interesting to know as
well is that your functions,

00:12:44.330 --> 00:12:49.280
they don't have to all
be in one single region.

00:12:49.280 --> 00:12:51.110
But you can have
functions in Europe.

00:12:51.110 --> 00:12:54.900
function in the US, within
the same GCP project.

00:12:54.900 --> 00:12:58.860
So if you want to have functions
that are closer to your users,

00:12:58.860 --> 00:13:00.110
you can do that.

00:13:00.110 --> 00:13:02.190
You have modern runtimes.

00:13:02.190 --> 00:13:05.480
So the newly announced
runtimes, node 8

00:13:05.480 --> 00:13:08.420
for using things
like async await

00:13:08.420 --> 00:13:11.630
to make your code easier
to read when handling

00:13:11.630 --> 00:13:13.460
the promises, et cetera.

00:13:13.460 --> 00:13:19.460
Python 3.7-- so that's the
very latest and greatest Python

00:13:19.460 --> 00:13:24.450
with a thin Flask framework
for defining your functions.

00:13:24.450 --> 00:13:28.540
And there are also more
languages coming soon.

00:13:28.540 --> 00:13:30.290
In terms of the coming
features, I already

00:13:30.290 --> 00:13:32.510
mentioned all the
error reporting,

00:13:32.510 --> 00:13:34.970
logging capabilities,
which is always nice

00:13:34.970 --> 00:13:36.560
when you're
developing debugging,

00:13:36.560 --> 00:13:39.080
and if you have got
some prediction issues

00:13:39.080 --> 00:13:42.990
you want to trace back
what actually happened.

00:13:42.990 --> 00:13:47.210
A very important aspect,
that's the built-in dependency

00:13:47.210 --> 00:13:48.560
resolution in the Cloud.

00:13:48.560 --> 00:13:50.820
So you don't have
to, for example,

00:13:50.820 --> 00:13:55.010
when you use node modules,
some may require some libraries

00:13:55.010 --> 00:14:00.060
or need to be compiled because
there is some native code.

00:14:00.060 --> 00:14:02.362
So you don't have to
cross-compile things locally,

00:14:02.362 --> 00:14:04.070
upload the modules,
and things like that.

00:14:04.070 --> 00:14:07.600
It's really in the cloud that
this dependency resolution

00:14:07.600 --> 00:14:08.750
and build is being done.

00:14:08.750 --> 00:14:11.420
So it's one thing less to
think about-- really just

00:14:11.420 --> 00:14:13.790
focus on defining
your dependencies

00:14:13.790 --> 00:14:17.660
and writing your code.

00:14:17.660 --> 00:14:19.205
I think there was
something else.

00:14:22.100 --> 00:14:28.660
Oh, yeah, when I was
mentioning dependencies,

00:14:28.660 --> 00:14:31.910
your French friends are
actually running on uBuntu image

00:14:31.910 --> 00:14:34.850
under the hood, and there
are several libraries

00:14:34.850 --> 00:14:36.650
that are already pre-installed.

00:14:36.650 --> 00:14:41.930
So, for example, things like
FFmpeg for dealing with videos,

00:14:41.930 --> 00:14:46.340
ImageMagick if you want to do
some stuff with your pictures.

00:14:46.340 --> 00:14:49.460
But you also have all the set
of libraries that are required

00:14:49.460 --> 00:14:51.224
to run Chrome in headless mode.

00:14:51.224 --> 00:14:53.390
So, for example, if you
want to have a function that

00:14:53.390 --> 00:14:56.510
takes screenshots or generates
PDFs from a particular web

00:14:56.510 --> 00:15:01.070
page, if you want to do some
kind of end-to-end testing

00:15:01.070 --> 00:15:05.990
where your function actually
controls a browser, Chrome

00:15:05.990 --> 00:15:08.240
Headless browser, you
can do that easily

00:15:08.240 --> 00:15:09.680
with Cloud Functions today.

00:15:12.560 --> 00:15:16.550
So the newest capabilities that
have been announced as well.

00:15:16.550 --> 00:15:19.770
That's the VPC and VPN access.

00:15:19.770 --> 00:15:21.740
So you can say, when you
deploy your function,

00:15:21.740 --> 00:15:26.010
that your function is tied
to a particular VPN network.

00:15:26.010 --> 00:15:29.510
So what that means is
that, let's say you already

00:15:29.510 --> 00:15:32.060
have some VMs running
on Compute Engine,

00:15:32.060 --> 00:15:35.090
your function is able
to access those VMs

00:15:35.090 --> 00:15:36.200
and chat with those VMs.

00:15:36.200 --> 00:15:39.290
So it's not two
separate worlds anymore.

00:15:39.290 --> 00:15:41.600
In addition to that,
related to security,

00:15:41.600 --> 00:15:45.810
you have also IM role binding.

00:15:45.810 --> 00:15:48.320
So if you want to
say, OK, this function

00:15:48.320 --> 00:15:52.710
should be called only by
that role or et cetera,

00:15:52.710 --> 00:15:54.510
this is now possible.

00:15:54.510 --> 00:15:56.210
So instead of having
a function that

00:15:56.210 --> 00:16:01.640
is publicly available in the
cloud via HTTP, for example,

00:16:01.640 --> 00:16:07.140
you have finer grain control
now in terms of secure access.

00:16:07.140 --> 00:16:09.630
We also introduced
scaling controls.

00:16:09.630 --> 00:16:14.090
So it's especially for
limiting, actually,

00:16:14.090 --> 00:16:17.010
the number of instances of
one particular function.

00:16:17.010 --> 00:16:20.970
So this can be useful for things
like cost control, of course.

00:16:20.970 --> 00:16:25.610
But also, let's say you have a
function that calls a database,

00:16:25.610 --> 00:16:28.490
instead of overloading
the database with too many

00:16:28.490 --> 00:16:31.400
connections, if you know you
cannot have more than 100

00:16:31.400 --> 00:16:35.660
connections, you can say, OK,
I don't want more than 100

00:16:35.660 --> 00:16:40.280
functions to be running to
avoid the problem of draining

00:16:40.280 --> 00:16:44.150
the connections and having
connections issue with

00:16:44.150 --> 00:16:46.060
your database.

00:16:46.060 --> 00:16:49.680
Before it was GA--

00:16:49.680 --> 00:16:52.400
well, no, it is already GA.

00:16:52.400 --> 00:16:53.390
it's not yet.

00:16:53.390 --> 00:16:56.570
So all this stuff, except
environment variables,

00:16:56.570 --> 00:16:59.300
which are already
available, this stuff

00:16:59.300 --> 00:17:01.950
is coming the end of the year.

00:17:01.950 --> 00:17:04.670
So what I wanted to say about
the Cloud SQL direct connect

00:17:04.670 --> 00:17:08.510
is that so far, and
as of today, you

00:17:08.510 --> 00:17:10.579
had to go through the
internet if you wanted

00:17:10.579 --> 00:17:12.740
to access a Cloud SQL instance.

00:17:12.740 --> 00:17:18.450
But now with the upcoming Direct
SQL, Direct Connect Cloud SQL--

00:17:18.450 --> 00:17:22.560
oh, I mixed the
words, you get it--

00:17:22.560 --> 00:17:26.040
you are able to go
through the Google network

00:17:26.040 --> 00:17:29.130
directly without going
outside through the internet

00:17:29.130 --> 00:17:32.130
to get access to your database.

00:17:32.130 --> 00:17:34.810
Last but not least,
environment variables--

00:17:34.810 --> 00:17:37.980
so with functions, you're able
to define your environment

00:17:37.980 --> 00:17:38.560
variables.

00:17:38.560 --> 00:17:40.050
For example, if
you want to define

00:17:40.050 --> 00:17:44.130
different kind of environments,
prot, dev, staging,

00:17:44.130 --> 00:17:48.210
or if you have any pieces
of configuration that

00:17:48.210 --> 00:17:50.340
shouldn't really be stored
in your source code,

00:17:50.340 --> 00:17:53.025
in your source tree,
and your repository,

00:17:53.025 --> 00:17:55.950
it can be passed as
environment variables.

00:17:55.950 --> 00:17:58.530
And we'll see an
example in the demo

00:17:58.530 --> 00:18:02.730
where I am actually using
environment variables.

00:18:02.730 --> 00:18:04.800
All right, that's
about it for functions.

00:18:04.800 --> 00:18:07.289
Let's see a bit more
about App Engine.

00:18:07.289 --> 00:18:08.580
ALEXIS MOUSSINE-POUCHKINE: Yep.

00:18:08.580 --> 00:18:12.510
Let's switch gears and
talk about App Engine.

00:18:12.510 --> 00:18:15.360
Actually, no, the demo will
come in just a little bit later.

00:18:15.360 --> 00:18:16.580
Can we go back to slides?

00:18:20.160 --> 00:18:22.800
So the fundamental difference
with Cloud Functions

00:18:22.800 --> 00:18:24.510
here is, well, in the name.

00:18:24.510 --> 00:18:27.330
The unit of computing of
deployment, if you will,

00:18:27.330 --> 00:18:29.010
here, is an app.

00:18:29.010 --> 00:18:32.790
So an app, obviously, is
coarser grain than a function.

00:18:32.790 --> 00:18:36.300
It might be interesting if
you're moving from a monolith

00:18:36.300 --> 00:18:38.820
to the cloud--

00:18:38.820 --> 00:18:42.210
I mean moving from a monolith
to function-based programming,

00:18:42.210 --> 00:18:44.340
and full microservices
might be a bridge

00:18:44.340 --> 00:18:46.320
too far for many people.

00:18:46.320 --> 00:18:48.540
Instead, App Engine
may be a better fit.

00:18:48.540 --> 00:18:50.646
And still in the
realm of serverless

00:18:50.646 --> 00:18:52.520
where we take care of
a lot of things for you

00:18:52.520 --> 00:18:55.230
as we already said.

00:18:55.230 --> 00:18:59.310
But as we will see, you
are still, in App Engine,

00:18:59.310 --> 00:19:01.770
able to structure your code.

00:19:01.770 --> 00:19:03.760
And that, we believe,
is important.

00:19:03.760 --> 00:19:08.520
So the unit is the application,
the trigger is HTTP,

00:19:08.520 --> 00:19:10.710
and it's best used
for migrating,

00:19:10.710 --> 00:19:12.930
we believe, existing
applications, and, generally

00:19:12.930 --> 00:19:16.140
speaking, for HTTP services.

00:19:16.140 --> 00:19:19.320
Most developers will have
preferred languages--

00:19:19.320 --> 00:19:21.620
I know I do--

00:19:21.620 --> 00:19:24.060
and expect to use them
with no constraints.

00:19:24.060 --> 00:19:26.670
Like, don't tell me to
use that proprietary PI.

00:19:26.670 --> 00:19:29.370
Don't tell me you cannot
use that set of libraries.

00:19:29.370 --> 00:19:32.250
I would like to be able to
use this in what we call

00:19:32.250 --> 00:19:36.240
idiomatic ways,
which is any habit

00:19:36.240 --> 00:19:39.570
that I have I would like to be
able to take this to the Cloud.

00:19:39.570 --> 00:19:40.570
We'll come back to this.

00:19:40.570 --> 00:19:42.522
This is really important.

00:19:42.522 --> 00:19:43.980
To deploy your
application, there's

00:19:43.980 --> 00:19:45.600
really no configuration needed.

00:19:45.600 --> 00:19:49.650
It's a G cloud or SDK
command line, app deploy.

00:19:49.650 --> 00:19:53.190
And it will look at your
artifacts, your application,

00:19:53.190 --> 00:19:54.830
and it will deploy this.

00:19:54.830 --> 00:19:58.320
And once deployed,
similar as with functions,

00:19:58.320 --> 00:20:02.670
you'll be able to auto
scale from zero to a lot,

00:20:02.670 --> 00:20:04.680
and back to zero.

00:20:04.680 --> 00:20:06.390
Billing is done accordingly.

00:20:06.390 --> 00:20:10.040
No traffic, no cost.

00:20:10.040 --> 00:20:13.420
So App Engine today--

00:20:13.420 --> 00:20:16.210
App Engine is 10 years old.

00:20:16.210 --> 00:20:18.700
So that's a decade
of a lot of things.

00:20:18.700 --> 00:20:22.480
And some of you might have seen
App Engine not evolve quite

00:20:22.480 --> 00:20:24.290
a bit for the last few years.

00:20:24.290 --> 00:20:27.880
And the reason for that is that
the team has been hard at work

00:20:27.880 --> 00:20:33.040
on a new infrastructure and
a new sandbox technology.

00:20:33.040 --> 00:20:35.380
Which enables a lot
of things, security

00:20:35.380 --> 00:20:37.330
being the most
important, but for you

00:20:37.330 --> 00:20:39.670
developers there are a few
things coming with that as

00:20:39.670 --> 00:20:40.640
well.

00:20:40.640 --> 00:20:43.810
So first of all, we have,
generally available, the Java 8

00:20:43.810 --> 00:20:48.580
runtime, which enables not only
Java 8 and all the features

00:20:48.580 --> 00:20:51.730
that come with it, such as
lambda expressions, pipeline

00:20:51.730 --> 00:20:55.060
streams, but also all the
languages running on the JVM,

00:20:55.060 --> 00:21:00.160
such as Scala, Kotlin, Groovy.

00:21:00.160 --> 00:21:03.580
So that is really something
that a lot of people use.

00:21:03.580 --> 00:21:06.650
The second thing is, just as
with functions, we support

00:21:06.650 --> 00:21:09.130
Node 8, currently in beta.

00:21:09.130 --> 00:21:13.930
In the next 30 days you will
have access to Python and PHP

00:21:13.930 --> 00:21:18.310
with very modern and
recent versions as well.

00:21:18.310 --> 00:21:21.870
And more features, more
languages, more updates

00:21:21.870 --> 00:21:24.160
are coming soon.

00:21:24.160 --> 00:21:26.250
We also have a new
instance scheduler,

00:21:26.250 --> 00:21:28.920
that logic that says how
many, and how quickly,

00:21:28.920 --> 00:21:31.650
and how often I need to
spin up new instances,

00:21:31.650 --> 00:21:36.390
and what is the logic behind
scaling down, which is really,

00:21:36.390 --> 00:21:40.800
I would think, the secret sauce
behind App Engine, is new.

00:21:40.800 --> 00:21:44.520
And that was released
a couple of months ago.

00:21:44.520 --> 00:21:46.410
We believe its more
predictable-- so you

00:21:46.410 --> 00:21:49.560
understand why it's doing
the things it's doing.

00:21:49.560 --> 00:21:53.010
It offers better utilization
with less cold starts--

00:21:53.010 --> 00:21:55.970
so when you hit an instance
that is hit for the first time

00:21:55.970 --> 00:21:58.440
there's less of that.

00:21:58.440 --> 00:22:00.930
And it offers new
settings to balance

00:22:00.930 --> 00:22:03.690
costs versus performance.

00:22:03.690 --> 00:22:06.030
And we believe we have
faster deployments, which

00:22:06.030 --> 00:22:07.110
is always important.

00:22:07.110 --> 00:22:09.270
We do a lot of things
when we deploy.

00:22:09.270 --> 00:22:11.285
We're deploying
requires a cluster.

00:22:11.285 --> 00:22:13.410
I mean the cluster is there,
you just don't see it.

00:22:13.410 --> 00:22:16.860
So we need to do a lot
of checks before we

00:22:16.860 --> 00:22:20.340
can say that the app
is live and running.

00:22:20.340 --> 00:22:22.280
And we believe we
can do that better.

00:22:22.280 --> 00:22:24.030
And if you're in the
health care industry,

00:22:24.030 --> 00:22:27.240
you'll be happy to know that
as of, again, a few months ago,

00:22:27.240 --> 00:22:31.380
we are now HIPAA compliant.

00:22:31.380 --> 00:22:33.390
Now, let's go back
to this idiomatic way

00:22:33.390 --> 00:22:35.040
of developing apps.

00:22:35.040 --> 00:22:38.040
We call this the second
generation idiomatic runtimes.

00:22:38.040 --> 00:22:41.190
And this is enabled by a
technology called gVisor.

00:22:41.190 --> 00:22:43.860
Anybody has heard of
gVisor here before?

00:22:43.860 --> 00:22:44.769
Just a few people.

00:22:44.769 --> 00:22:46.060
This is really cool technology.

00:22:46.060 --> 00:22:48.990
You don't have to really
understand how it works,

00:22:48.990 --> 00:22:52.330
you can just reap the benefits.

00:22:52.330 --> 00:22:54.750
It is an open source
technology that we've

00:22:54.750 --> 00:22:59.400
announced at the last
CubeCon-- so a few months ago.

00:22:59.400 --> 00:23:02.220
It is a new sandbox
technology which

00:23:02.220 --> 00:23:05.890
offers this idiomatic
developer experience.

00:23:05.890 --> 00:23:09.190
You can use any API, any
library that you want,

00:23:09.190 --> 00:23:10.770
and it should just work.

00:23:10.770 --> 00:23:13.860
Any module from the
official Node.js package

00:23:13.860 --> 00:23:17.230
manager such as headless Chrome,
which Guillaume mentioned,

00:23:17.230 --> 00:23:21.630
any Java jar library,
any Python library.

00:23:21.630 --> 00:23:23.790
You do not need to change
your code and the way

00:23:23.790 --> 00:23:25.410
it uses those libraries.

00:23:25.410 --> 00:23:29.490
At most you should have
to add maybe some metadata

00:23:29.490 --> 00:23:32.430
to your application in the
form of some YAML or XML, just

00:23:32.430 --> 00:23:35.160
to describe it, to give
it a version number,

00:23:35.160 --> 00:23:37.660
and that's really it.

00:23:37.660 --> 00:23:39.960
Note, by the way, that
everything I'm describing here,

00:23:39.960 --> 00:23:42.420
all the benefits
gVisor, is something

00:23:42.420 --> 00:23:45.035
we're using both in Cloud
Functions and in App Engine.

00:23:48.440 --> 00:23:50.570
Now, this is not a
new feature, but I

00:23:50.570 --> 00:23:53.210
think it's important we
spend a minute on it.

00:23:53.210 --> 00:23:55.790
App Engine, because
application can

00:23:55.790 --> 00:23:58.880
have multiple distinct
layers or modules,

00:23:58.880 --> 00:24:03.740
say a front-end, some business
logic, some data access layers,

00:24:03.740 --> 00:24:06.830
some admin piece, as
you'll see in the demo,

00:24:06.830 --> 00:24:08.630
it's interesting
to be able to split

00:24:08.630 --> 00:24:13.040
your application in different
modules or services.

00:24:13.040 --> 00:24:17.480
In App Engine you can
do that, of course.

00:24:17.480 --> 00:24:19.637
Each service has a version.

00:24:19.637 --> 00:24:21.470
Potentially, all those
versions, by the way,

00:24:21.470 --> 00:24:22.980
are active at the same time.

00:24:22.980 --> 00:24:26.240
And you'll see why
that could be useful.

00:24:26.240 --> 00:24:28.910
And each version can
be scaled independently

00:24:28.910 --> 00:24:30.440
from the other one.

00:24:30.440 --> 00:24:33.260
You probably don't have the same
scaling needs for some admin

00:24:33.260 --> 00:24:36.470
back-end with two
people accessing it,

00:24:36.470 --> 00:24:41.210
and the front-end, which
is for the world to use.

00:24:41.210 --> 00:24:44.360
So we believe it's a great
fit for microservices.

00:24:44.360 --> 00:24:47.540
Each service can also be
written in a different language.

00:24:47.540 --> 00:24:50.077
There's really no
constraints there.

00:24:50.077 --> 00:24:52.160
And if that makes sense
for you, you can certainly

00:24:52.160 --> 00:24:54.120
take advantage of that.

00:24:54.120 --> 00:24:57.470
But maybe more powerful,
this built-in feature,

00:24:57.470 --> 00:25:00.830
multiple services
active at the same time,

00:25:00.830 --> 00:25:05.540
enables things such as service
level upgrades, A/B testing,

00:25:05.540 --> 00:25:07.640
canary releases, all
of that with something

00:25:07.640 --> 00:25:10.280
we call traffic splitting.

00:25:10.280 --> 00:25:13.760
Traffic splitting is not
a new feature either,

00:25:13.760 --> 00:25:16.230
but it proves to be
extremely powerful,

00:25:16.230 --> 00:25:21.110
and I would say trivial,
or very easy, to enable.

00:25:21.110 --> 00:25:26.610
Once you've given a
module, a service, a name,

00:25:26.610 --> 00:25:27.860
which is free-form.

00:25:27.860 --> 00:25:29.300
You could call it 1235.

00:25:29.300 --> 00:25:32.950
You could call it beta, green,
red, blue, whatever you'd like.

00:25:32.950 --> 00:25:34.200
You do this in a config file.

00:25:34.200 --> 00:25:40.310
Once you have that, you can
start addressing and sending

00:25:40.310 --> 00:25:42.830
parts of the traffic
to a new version.

00:25:45.440 --> 00:25:48.800
And this is based on IP address
on cookie, or completely

00:25:48.800 --> 00:25:50.730
at random.

00:25:50.730 --> 00:25:53.240
So this allows you to do
something like A/B testing.

00:25:53.240 --> 00:25:55.340
Maybe you will be
offering shipping costs

00:25:55.340 --> 00:25:59.690
to 50% of your users, or you'll
be offering an general discount

00:25:59.690 --> 00:26:01.880
on your goods to the
other 50%, and you'll

00:26:01.880 --> 00:26:04.790
see which one works best.

00:26:04.790 --> 00:26:06.380
So that's something you can do.

00:26:06.380 --> 00:26:08.710
And of course you can also
do canary releases-- deploy

00:26:08.710 --> 00:26:14.510
a new app to a few percentage of
your users, see how it behaves,

00:26:14.510 --> 00:26:18.260
and as you increase confidence
and you see no errors,

00:26:18.260 --> 00:26:20.990
you start sending more
load, more traffic,

00:26:20.990 --> 00:26:23.570
and eventually you move
the entire traffic to it.

00:26:23.570 --> 00:26:25.430
Or you go back
because you've found

00:26:25.430 --> 00:26:27.650
something that's really bad.

00:26:27.650 --> 00:26:31.040
No code changes,
no config changes

00:26:31.040 --> 00:26:35.810
in terms of changing
any XML or YAML file.

00:26:35.810 --> 00:26:38.540
This is all done very
simply from the command line

00:26:38.540 --> 00:26:40.430
or from the UI.

00:26:40.430 --> 00:26:42.200
And, of course,
traces and logs can

00:26:42.200 --> 00:26:44.017
be used to filter by version.

00:26:44.017 --> 00:26:45.850
And this is where you
can see that something

00:26:45.850 --> 00:26:51.810
is going good, or not so good
and you might need to rollback.

00:26:51.810 --> 00:26:53.290
So for those of
you that have been

00:26:53.290 --> 00:26:56.110
using App Engine
for several years,

00:26:56.110 --> 00:26:58.210
you might have seen that
a number of App Engine's

00:26:58.210 --> 00:27:02.200
specific features have graduated
into a standalone product.

00:27:02.200 --> 00:27:05.380
The most famous of them
has been Datastore.

00:27:05.380 --> 00:27:07.750
Which might seem crazy,
but back in the days

00:27:07.750 --> 00:27:10.960
you had to use App Engine to
access this amazing no SQL

00:27:10.960 --> 00:27:13.350
back-end that we have.

00:27:13.350 --> 00:27:18.010
There are many more services
that we'd like to point out,

00:27:18.010 --> 00:27:20.180
but there's two that
we like to point out.

00:27:20.180 --> 00:27:21.340
They are fairly new.

00:27:21.340 --> 00:27:24.220
And that can be used from App
Engine, from Cloud Functions,

00:27:24.220 --> 00:27:28.900
or maybe even if you're
still running on a VM

00:27:28.900 --> 00:27:31.180
on Compute Engine,
that you can use

00:27:31.180 --> 00:27:34.480
and that are now
standalone GCP product.

00:27:34.480 --> 00:27:37.840
The first one is
called Cloud Tasks,

00:27:37.840 --> 00:27:39.940
which will soon be in beta.

00:27:39.940 --> 00:27:44.090
I think the official thing
is in the next 30 days.

00:27:44.090 --> 00:27:46.180
So stay tuned.

00:27:46.180 --> 00:27:48.430
Initially, this was an
App Engine feature--

00:27:48.430 --> 00:27:50.980
task queues.

00:27:50.980 --> 00:27:53.050
And it's coming
soon as a standalone

00:27:53.050 --> 00:27:54.310
and fully managed service.

00:27:54.310 --> 00:27:57.790
It will take care of
executing, dispatching,

00:27:57.790 --> 00:28:01.750
and delivering a large
number of distributed tasks.

00:28:01.750 --> 00:28:04.330
Using this, you can
perform work asynchronously

00:28:04.330 --> 00:28:08.680
outside of the user requests,
and process requests

00:28:08.680 --> 00:28:09.890
in batches.

00:28:09.890 --> 00:28:13.252
So this is a great way to
scale your architecture.

00:28:13.252 --> 00:28:14.710
The main architectural
benefit here

00:28:14.710 --> 00:28:19.420
is really to be able to decouple
the user requests from scaling

00:28:19.420 --> 00:28:22.054
all those microservices.

00:28:22.054 --> 00:28:23.470
In the beta release
you'll support

00:28:23.470 --> 00:28:25.990
for Python, Java, and Node.

00:28:25.990 --> 00:28:30.030
And you'll be able to set things
such as rate limits, retry,

00:28:30.030 --> 00:28:31.480
configs.

00:28:31.480 --> 00:28:33.130
And this will be, I
believe, something

00:28:33.130 --> 00:28:36.820
that's fairly useful for
building scalable microservices

00:28:36.820 --> 00:28:39.580
architectures.

00:28:39.580 --> 00:28:42.070
Next is Google Cloud Scheduler.

00:28:42.070 --> 00:28:46.000
Well, this is really
chron in the cloud.

00:28:46.000 --> 00:28:49.060
Nothing super fancy, but this is
something that a lot of people

00:28:49.060 --> 00:28:49.750
need.

00:28:49.750 --> 00:28:53.350
And we believe that what we have
now with Cloud Scheduler, which

00:28:53.350 --> 00:28:55.960
we'll show briefly in
the demo, is the ability

00:28:55.960 --> 00:28:58.570
to configure the
regularly scheduled tasks

00:28:58.570 --> 00:29:03.280
and have them operate at
those regular intervals.

00:29:03.280 --> 00:29:06.400
So common use cases are,
being able to send an email

00:29:06.400 --> 00:29:10.930
reports on a daily basis, maybe
update some cache data every 10

00:29:10.930 --> 00:29:16.310
minutes, or update some
summary information every hour.

00:29:16.310 --> 00:29:18.940
This is also probably
the most elegant solution

00:29:18.940 --> 00:29:24.280
to the frequently requested
feature of invoking functions

00:29:24.280 --> 00:29:25.810
at regular intervals.

00:29:25.810 --> 00:29:28.480
So again, this is not
tied to a product,

00:29:28.480 --> 00:29:30.205
this is a product
of it's own, and can

00:29:30.205 --> 00:29:35.410
be used to trigger jobs
and manage all of them

00:29:35.410 --> 00:29:38.350
in one place, rather than
have and reinvent this chron

00:29:38.350 --> 00:29:42.180
feature in different parts
of your architecture.

00:29:42.180 --> 00:29:46.620
And, of course, you can hit
HTTP endpoints, Pun/Sub topic,

00:29:46.620 --> 00:29:54.100
or relative URL handlers
to hit App Engine apps.

00:29:54.100 --> 00:29:57.990
And this will be made
available in the next 30 days.

