00:32:09.080 --> 00:32:10.290
So what's happening?

00:32:10.290 --> 00:32:19.840
So a user takes a picture via
the web page via JavaScript,

00:32:19.840 --> 00:32:23.720
the picture is sent to
the main App Engine,

00:32:23.720 --> 00:32:28.600
so the front end, which
defines a web API in where

00:32:28.600 --> 00:32:30.980
I can post my picture.

00:32:30.980 --> 00:32:36.710
When that picture is
taken and sent to my app,

00:32:36.710 --> 00:32:39.215
we are going to store that
picture in Cloud Storage.

00:32:42.800 --> 00:32:46.010
And before I mention what
happens to the picture,

00:32:46.010 --> 00:32:50.690
we also add some metadata,
like the URL, basically,

00:32:50.690 --> 00:32:54.710
of the name of the
picture in the Datastore.

00:32:54.710 --> 00:32:58.410
When that picture
arrives on Cloud Storage

00:32:58.410 --> 00:33:00.800
a function is triggered.

00:33:00.800 --> 00:33:06.030
This function is going to call
the Vision API to get labels--

00:33:06.030 --> 00:33:08.930
so that's the tags you
see below the pictures--

00:33:08.930 --> 00:33:11.330
to get the color,
the dominant color--

00:33:11.330 --> 00:33:15.350
that's why the pictures were
framed with some colors.

00:33:15.350 --> 00:33:20.270
And we're also using the filter,
the save search annotation,

00:33:20.270 --> 00:33:23.670
which tells you if
the content contains

00:33:23.670 --> 00:33:29.060
adult, racy, violent,
medical content,

00:33:29.060 --> 00:33:30.800
and that kind of stuff.

00:33:30.800 --> 00:33:34.580
When the Vision API
answers the request,

00:33:34.580 --> 00:33:39.560
the function actually adds
metadata in the datastore--

00:33:39.560 --> 00:33:42.830
so again, the labels,
color, filters, et cetera--

00:33:42.830 --> 00:33:46.520
in the entry we created when
we uploaded the picture.

00:33:46.520 --> 00:33:47.420
So that's one thing.

00:33:51.060 --> 00:33:57.050
So if you remember the popular
tags page, in the popular tags,

00:33:57.050 --> 00:34:02.010
so the way I implemented it, it
was a bit of an afterthought.

00:34:02.010 --> 00:34:04.730
I'm actually going
through all the metadata

00:34:04.730 --> 00:34:05.620
stored in datastores.

00:34:05.620 --> 00:34:09.894
So I'm going through all the
other lines in my data store.

00:34:09.894 --> 00:34:11.810
So it's not the most
efficient way to do that.

00:34:11.810 --> 00:34:14.810
So it's an expensive query
which takes time to run.

00:34:14.810 --> 00:34:18.469
Actually, for the few hundred
or thousands of pictures

00:34:18.469 --> 00:34:19.969
that I have, it's pretty fast.

00:34:19.969 --> 00:34:22.190
But let's imagine I've
got millions of pictures,

00:34:22.190 --> 00:34:24.460
and if I have to go
through all those pictures,

00:34:24.460 --> 00:34:26.929
the query might take a
little bit more time.

00:34:26.929 --> 00:34:30.020
So instead, what I'm doing
is that I'm taking actually

00:34:30.020 --> 00:34:33.560
a snapshot of the most frequent
pictures that I'm storing again

00:34:33.560 --> 00:34:34.670
in datastore.

00:34:34.670 --> 00:34:38.659
So that way I don't have
to do the costly operation,

00:34:38.659 --> 00:34:43.219
I just have to do a simple read
operation in the datastore,

00:34:43.219 --> 00:34:45.780
rather than going
through all the results.

00:34:45.780 --> 00:34:50.600
So App Engine for the front-end,
