00:42:12.150 --> 00:42:14.260
going to show you right now.

00:42:14.260 --> 00:42:17.070
So we saw some Java,
we saw some Go.

00:42:17.070 --> 00:42:21.120
So now, let's have a look at
some JavaScript with Node.

00:42:21.120 --> 00:42:25.740
So here-- and we're
receiving an event.

00:42:25.740 --> 00:42:30.390
The event is actually, in
terms of the data that's

00:42:30.390 --> 00:42:34.530
stored in that event object,
it contains the filename

00:42:34.530 --> 00:42:39.490
of the picture and the bucket
in which this picture is stored.

00:42:39.490 --> 00:42:41.670
So what I'm doing
there is that I'm

00:42:41.670 --> 00:42:44.350
going to prepare query
for the Vision API.

00:42:44.350 --> 00:42:45.690
So I will enable detection.

00:42:45.690 --> 00:42:47.310
I want the image
properties to be

00:42:47.310 --> 00:42:52.110
able to get the dominant color,
safe search for filtering.

00:42:52.110 --> 00:42:54.180
I'm calling the API here.

00:42:54.180 --> 00:42:59.670
Once I get the API
call is successful,

00:42:59.670 --> 00:43:04.260
I get through the JSON to
find the right information.

00:43:04.260 --> 00:43:08.730
And then I'm going
to store some updates

00:43:08.730 --> 00:43:10.900
to the metadata for the file.

00:43:10.900 --> 00:43:13.770
So I'm going to store the main
color, the labels, et cetera,

00:43:13.770 --> 00:43:18.780
back into the datastore,

00:43:18.780 --> 00:43:20.490
And the other one--

00:43:20.490 --> 00:43:24.030
so as I said, computing
the most frequent tags

00:43:24.030 --> 00:43:26.250
is an expensive operation.

00:43:26.250 --> 00:43:29.700
So I decided to do some
snapshots once every minute.

00:43:29.700 --> 00:43:34.580
So I defined a crumb of,
well, once every minute,

00:43:34.580 --> 00:43:36.120
please call my function.

00:43:36.120 --> 00:43:40.420
So this is the second
function, which is here.

00:43:40.420 --> 00:43:45.660
So first of all,
notice that I used

00:43:45.660 --> 00:43:48.460
the new feature,
environment variables, here.

00:43:48.460 --> 00:43:50.130
So I can access the
environment variable

00:43:50.130 --> 00:43:54.300
by using process dot nth dot
the name of my environment

00:43:54.300 --> 00:43:55.110
variable.

00:43:55.110 --> 00:43:56.800
So I have a default
value, just in case

00:43:56.800 --> 00:43:59.310
it's not passed as parameters.

00:43:59.310 --> 00:44:04.650
But if you look at
the functions here

00:44:04.650 --> 00:44:09.330
in the console, popular
tags, which is here,

00:44:09.330 --> 00:44:13.380
you can see down there,
environment variables,

00:44:13.380 --> 00:44:17.040
API endpoint, I defined
the URL of the endpoint.

00:44:17.040 --> 00:44:19.290
All right?

00:44:19.290 --> 00:44:20.450
What am I doing here?

00:44:20.450 --> 00:44:23.940
So I'm not really interested
in any data from the events,

00:44:23.940 --> 00:44:27.530
because it's really
just once every minute.

00:44:27.530 --> 00:44:30.540
So there's no data
that I actually need.

00:44:30.540 --> 00:44:34.440
And then I'm doing the
frequency calculation

00:44:34.440 --> 00:44:40.440
and storing the most frequent
tags in the datastore again.

00:44:40.440 --> 00:44:43.410
So that's about it for the code.

00:44:43.410 --> 00:44:48.480
So again, you see
previously there

00:44:48.480 --> 00:44:52.410
were some errors when I was
developing those functions.

00:44:52.410 --> 00:44:54.330
You can see the
actual usage there.

00:44:54.330 --> 00:44:56.730
So there are really
two functions--

00:44:56.730 --> 00:45:02.020
popular tags, which is
a message via a topic,

00:45:02.020 --> 00:45:05.700
Cloud Scheduler pushes a
message on the Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:45:05.700 --> 00:45:08.940
And here there's a bucket.

00:45:08.940 --> 00:45:11.910
That's the bucket where
I stored my pictures.

00:45:11.910 --> 00:45:14.560
And I'd like to
show you two things.

00:45:14.560 --> 00:45:17.570
So this morning, I did a load.

00:45:17.570 --> 00:45:20.640
So it was-- yeah, this
morning, so it's a bit small.

00:45:20.640 --> 00:45:22.320
But if you remember,
in the abstract,

00:45:22.320 --> 00:45:24.750
we said, yea, scale
from 0 to 1,000.

00:45:24.750 --> 00:45:26.120
So I did.

00:45:26.120 --> 00:45:28.920
I created some load
injectors to be

00:45:28.920 --> 00:45:31.990
able to try to go up to 1,000.

00:45:31.990 --> 00:45:34.340
So I can't zoom in
because it's gone,

00:45:34.340 --> 00:45:40.500
but I managed to go up
to 938 instances created

00:45:40.500 --> 00:45:44.820
in just a couple of minutes by
adding more load and more load

00:45:44.820 --> 00:45:46.440
injectors.

00:45:46.440 --> 00:45:51.870
And at some point, I really
had like almost 600 instances

00:45:51.870 --> 00:45:52.950
running.

00:45:52.950 --> 00:45:55.200
And the other thing
I wanted to mention,

00:45:55.200 --> 00:45:57.450
a feature that I
particularly like,

00:45:57.450 --> 00:46:00.720
when you look at the services--

00:46:00.720 --> 00:46:02.430
the version, sorry.

00:46:02.430 --> 00:46:05.650
I have two versions for
my application here.

00:46:05.650 --> 00:46:07.270
So this is the normal version.

00:46:07.270 --> 00:46:08.620
But I also have a gray version.

00:46:08.620 --> 00:46:12.090
So if I want to do some A/B
testing to see if users prefer

00:46:12.090 --> 00:46:18.810
a gray background instead of
white, here in the console,

00:46:18.810 --> 00:46:23.670
I can easily do split
traffic at random.

00:46:23.670 --> 00:46:25.440
I want to add a new version.

00:46:25.440 --> 00:46:28.380
And for example, I
want the gray version

00:46:28.380 --> 00:46:32.650
to get a bit of traffic.

00:46:32.650 --> 00:46:35.610
So I'm updating those settings,
or you can do it with G Cloud

00:46:35.610 --> 00:46:36.540
to come in line.

00:46:36.540 --> 00:46:41.310
And once in a while you should
see a different background.

00:46:41.310 --> 00:46:43.640
White, white, white.

00:46:43.640 --> 00:46:44.779
Ahh.

00:46:44.779 --> 00:46:45.570
Gray at some point?

00:46:45.570 --> 00:46:46.930
Yeah, gray at some point.

00:46:46.930 --> 00:46:49.110
So it's fairly easy to
do traffic splitting

00:46:49.110 --> 00:46:52.870
for A/B testing,
canary releases,

00:46:52.870 --> 00:46:56.130
green-blue
deployments, and so on.

00:46:56.130 --> 00:46:57.422
All right, that's it.

00:46:57.422 --> 00:46:58.755
Let's switch back to the slides.

00:47:01.430 --> 00:47:03.860
ALEXIS MOUSSINE-POUCHKINE:
Thank you, Guillaume.

00:47:03.860 --> 00:47:06.110
So let's recap quickly
what we've seen.

00:47:06.110 --> 00:47:07.820
We've seen an
application deployed

00:47:07.820 --> 00:47:10.490
across Cloud Functions
and App Engine,

00:47:10.490 --> 00:47:14.330
with multiple functions written
in JavaScript with Node,

00:47:14.330 --> 00:47:18.470
and one applique App Engine
app using multiple services.

00:47:18.470 --> 00:47:21.530
All of this, including
idiomatic language

00:47:21.530 --> 00:47:23.630
runtime, such as
Node, obviously,

00:47:23.630 --> 00:47:28.370
for the functions, Java 8,
and Go for the admin part.

00:47:28.370 --> 00:47:31.340
We've used multiple
GCP services, again,

00:47:31.340 --> 00:47:33.200
both Functions and App Engine.

00:47:33.200 --> 00:47:35.420
The reason you want
to use those services

00:47:35.420 --> 00:47:39.230
is that you're able to
access the power of GCP.

00:47:39.230 --> 00:47:42.290
We used the Vision API, which
is one of the main pieces that

00:47:42.290 --> 00:47:44.060
gets you all those tags.

00:47:44.060 --> 00:47:47.280
But we've used a number of
additional services as well.

00:47:47.280 --> 00:47:49.040
And things that we
haven't shown too much,

00:47:49.040 --> 00:47:50.748
but we have a reference
to in other talks

00:47:50.748 --> 00:47:54.020
that we encourage to go
to, is how Stackdriver

00:47:54.020 --> 00:47:57.620
aggregates all the logs from all
the parts of your application

00:47:57.620 --> 00:47:59.870
and provides you with very
interesting information,

00:47:59.870 --> 00:48:03.350
both for developing and for
managing the application.

00:48:03.350 --> 00:48:06.440
Giving away
responsibility of scaling

00:48:06.440 --> 00:48:09.500
the application doesn't mean
giving away all the ops job

00:48:09.500 --> 00:48:12.260
and keeping you from seeing
what's really Happening--

00:48:12.260 --> 00:48:13.912
Quite the opposite, in fact.

00:48:13.912 --> 00:48:15.620
And of course, this
was fully serverless.

00:48:15.620 --> 00:48:17.230
No server was
harmed in this demo.

00:48:19.880 --> 00:48:21.950
Now, what you've
seen is that we're

00:48:21.950 --> 00:48:24.140
able to take your
artifacts, whether they

00:48:24.140 --> 00:48:28.040
come in the form of a function,
a collection of functions,

00:48:28.040 --> 00:48:32.810
an app with one or multiple
modules, and that's great.

00:48:32.810 --> 00:48:36.110
And we do this, and we
take those artifacts

00:48:36.110 --> 00:48:40.670
and we auto scale them,
and it's a per use model.

00:48:40.670 --> 00:48:43.160
Now, increasingly people
are packaging their code

00:48:43.160 --> 00:48:46.350
into containers for
various reasons.

00:48:46.350 --> 00:48:50.210
So what if we were able to offer
you, the developer, the choice

00:48:50.210 --> 00:48:53.090
of taking not just functions
or apps as deployment units,

00:48:53.090 --> 00:48:55.070
but also containers
and still offer

00:48:55.070 --> 00:48:59.930
that serverless operational
model with auto scalability

00:48:59.930 --> 00:49:01.440
and pay per use?

00:49:01.440 --> 00:49:03.530
So here are a couple
of upcoming options

00:49:03.530 --> 00:49:05.030
that we're excited
to share with you

00:49:05.030 --> 00:49:07.940
and that were
announced and covered

00:49:07.940 --> 00:49:09.650
this morning in the keynote.

00:49:09.650 --> 00:49:13.700
The first one is called
serverless containers.

00:49:13.700 --> 00:49:15.530
Imagine we have
containers that are

00:49:15.530 --> 00:49:20.272
stateless that comply to some
requests response signature.

00:49:20.272 --> 00:49:21.980
The promise here of
serverless containers

00:49:21.980 --> 00:49:24.170
is to offer that
auto scalability,

00:49:24.170 --> 00:49:27.710
health checks for applications
packaged in those containers

00:49:27.710 --> 00:49:30.680
with arbitrary libraries.

00:49:30.680 --> 00:49:34.550
So imagine a random
or any Docker file

00:49:34.550 --> 00:49:38.720
with any base image, an
arbitrary set of libraries

00:49:38.720 --> 00:49:42.920
or language runtime, be able
to deploy those with a command

00:49:42.920 --> 00:49:46.220
that you are familiar with,
G Cloud Functions deploy,

00:49:46.220 --> 00:49:48.530
and simply adding this
option of pointing

00:49:48.530 --> 00:49:50.870
to that particular
container that,

00:49:50.870 --> 00:49:53.060
again, needs to be stateless.

00:49:53.060 --> 00:49:55.070
Well, if you do
that, our job will

00:49:55.070 --> 00:49:57.860
be to provide you that
serverless environment where

00:49:57.860 --> 00:49:59.960
we auto scale, and do
all the health checks,

00:49:59.960 --> 00:50:02.450
and all the monitoring
that is required.

00:50:02.450 --> 00:50:06.380
No servers, and still pay
only while the code runs.

00:50:06.380 --> 00:50:08.990
If you'd like to sign up for
the EAP that is coming before

00:50:08.990 --> 00:50:14.270
the end of this year
g.co/serverlesscontainers.

00:50:14.270 --> 00:50:17.900
And this is G Cloud
Functions deploy.

00:50:17.900 --> 00:50:21.710
Now, a second option is the
serverless add-on for GKE.

00:50:21.710 --> 00:50:23.790
And this was also
discussed this morning.

00:50:23.790 --> 00:50:27.470
So whether we're talking
GKE, which we know and love

00:50:27.470 --> 00:50:32.630
in Google Cloud Platform,
or the GKE on-prem, which

00:50:32.630 --> 00:50:35.930
was just announced as
well, here what we have is

00:50:35.930 --> 00:50:39.380
a one-step install
technology built on Knative.

00:50:39.380 --> 00:50:42.890
If you haven't seen Knative, you
probably want to look at this.

00:50:42.890 --> 00:50:46.850
It's an open source
project that's

00:50:46.850 --> 00:50:52.040
helping make those artifacts
that you deploy portable.

00:50:52.040 --> 00:50:54.260
And in this case,
we used this, and we

00:50:54.260 --> 00:50:57.860
built the serverless add-on
on for GKE on Knative.

00:50:57.860 --> 00:51:00.470
And what it allows
is for you to deploy,

00:51:00.470 --> 00:51:06.830
this time with a new
artifact, the deploy with not

00:51:06.830 --> 00:51:12.450
only the container but also
the Kubernetes cluster,

00:51:12.450 --> 00:51:15.650
whether it's on-prem or GKE,
that you would like to target.

00:51:15.650 --> 00:51:17.810
And the reason we'd
like to do this,

00:51:17.810 --> 00:51:19.340
or to offer this
as an option, is

00:51:19.340 --> 00:51:22.700
if you were to reuse an
infrastructure that you already

00:51:22.700 --> 00:51:24.470
have, that you already know.

00:51:24.470 --> 00:51:26.990
Or maybe you would like to
have some custom VM sizes

00:51:26.990 --> 00:51:31.220
in that GKE cluster or GPUs.

00:51:31.220 --> 00:51:33.740
So with all those reasons we
have yet another option for you

00:51:33.740 --> 00:51:38.210
to deploy serverless
workloads, this time on GKE.

00:51:38.210 --> 00:51:40.020
So it's time to
scale down to zero.

00:51:40.020 --> 00:51:40.510
GUILLAUME LAFORGE: Yep.

00:51:40.510 --> 00:51:41.780
ALEXIS MOUSSINE-POUCHKINE: So
Guillaume want to thank you.

00:51:41.780 --> 00:51:42.140
GUILLAUME LAFORGE: Thank you.

00:51:42.140 --> 00:51:44.598
ALEXIS MOUSSINE-POUCHKINE: But
we need quickly to conclude.

00:51:44.598 --> 00:51:48.170
GUILLAUME LAFORGE: So whenever
you've got small units of code

00:51:48.170 --> 00:51:50.750
that you need in an
event-driven approach,

00:51:50.750 --> 00:51:52.850
Functions are
definitely a great fit

00:51:52.850 --> 00:51:57.320
for bigger business logic,
long running web apps, web API,

00:51:57.320 --> 00:51:58.340
or back-ends.

00:51:58.340 --> 00:52:03.590
App Engine is really awesome
and scales Snapchat-style,

00:52:03.590 --> 00:52:07.040
and serverless add-on for when
you need a more portability,

00:52:07.040 --> 00:52:09.590
more control on where
you are actually running,

00:52:09.590 --> 00:52:12.800
or if you need special
resources like GPUs,

00:52:12.800 --> 00:52:14.160
or that kind of stuff.

00:52:14.160 --> 00:52:15.350
So it's up to you.

00:52:15.350 --> 00:52:18.200
And you can mix
everything together.

00:52:18.200 --> 00:52:21.890
There are some more
sessions happening tomorrow.

00:52:21.890 --> 00:52:25.689
So thanks again for your
attention, and see you soon.

00:52:25.689 --> 00:52:27.230
ALEXIS MOUSSINE-POUCHKINE:
Thank you.

